Description: v4l2: keep polling the stateful decoder after POLLERR
 A memory-to-memory V4L2 device returns POLLERR from poll(), immediately
 rather than blocking, when for each queue that queue is not streaming, or
 has had vb2_queue_error() called on it, or has nothing queued. The last of
 those is normal here: the CAPTURE queue is small (on the MMAP path,
 reference frames plus two) and its buffers are mostly downstream at any
 moment. WaitOnceForEvents() treated POLLERR as unhandled and returned, and
 only the next Decode() re-armed the poll. After V4L2_DEC_CMD_STOP there is
 no next Decode(), so the flush never finished: a looping <video> froze just
 before the end of every clip. bcm2835-codec hits it at every clip end
 because it forwards the stop as an MMAL EOS buffer, and the EOS comes back
 on a CAPTURE buffer, so the LAST buffer can only be produced by a QBUF that
 happens after the poll has given up.
 .
 The older V4L2Device stack solved exactly this: V4L2DevicePoller documents
 that poll() returns immediately with nothing queued, and V4L2Queue's
 schedule_poll_cb exists so that every QBUF can wake the poller. The
 stateful decoder passes base::DoNothing() for it. Use it: every QBUF, and
 every DQBUF that leaves buffers queued or returns EAGAIN/EPIPE, arms
 monitoring unless a poll task is already pending. WaitOnceForEvents() now
 reports every exit with the revents it saw, and that report re-arms when
 nothing else did and the driver still owns buffers, which also covers two
 paths that never re-armed before: a LAST buffer dequeued while the OUTPUT
 queue still has work, and a failed VIDIOC_DQEVENT. A poll that returned
 with neither POLLIN nor POLLPRI returns immediately, so consecutive re-arms
 after such a poll are capped, and reaching the cap fails a pending flush
 and any unanswered Decode() instead of hanging them.
 .
 Task counts: the poll tasks upstream stacks one per Decode() are not
 de-duplicated (crrev.com/c/7850080 was reverted for doing that); the new
 arming is a no-op while a task is pending, so it adds at most one task in
 the places Decode() or InitializeCAPTUREQueue() queue buffers before their
 own unconditional re-arm, and the count does not grow. The count kept is of
 reports still expected, not of running tasks: cancelling leaves a task
 blocked in poll() that never reports. Not addressed: upstream's spin when
 V4L2_DEC_CMD_START fails after a LAST buffer (poll() then returns POLLIN
 for ever and the dequeue path re-arms on every pass); the cap here counts
 only polls without POLLIN or POLLPRI, so it does not bound that.
 .
 Upstream main still has the unhandled path. Nothing here is Pi-specific, so
 this should go upstream once tested; it will conflict textually with open
 CL 8181668, which reorders POLLPRI and POLLIN in the same function.
